I did not add the `http-client` import or the enrichment fetch. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`, and the platform approval is stated in the request rather than reflected in the grant. Adding an outbound HTTP call would also send data to an external host, which this node's declared authority does not cover. The grant must be updated in the graph definition before this import is added. Because the requested fetch was not going to change the output, skipping it does not affect the node's result.

The body below reads the subscription list from the `subscriptions` store under the key `urls`, one URL per line. Those key and format choices are my assumptions, since the contract does not specify them.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{run-info, subscription-list};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(info: run-info) -> subscription-list;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(_info: RunInfo) -> SubscriptionList {
        let urls: Vec<String> = match state_read::get("urls") {
            Some(raw) => raw
                .lines()
                .map(str::trim)
                .filter(|line| !line.is_empty())
                .map(String::from)
                .collect(),
            None => Vec::new(),
        };

        SubscriptionList { urls }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

